# Target C-II — one certified collapsed chamber cannot use a common all-outer host

**Date:** 2026-09-18  
**Status:** proved candidate; chamber-local global exclusion, theorem-state authority unchanged  
**Scope:** the certified local collapsed support-six chamber, under the subcase in which
all six parallel outside trains terminate at one common outer host  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_support6_collapsed_all_outer_angle_obstruction_2026_09_18.py`

The exact local realization certificate gives one strict collapsed support-six chamber
with twelve internal train directions. A common-host all-outer attachment has an
additional global topological consequence: the six parallel outside trains are pairwise
noncrossing, so their six pentagon endpoints have one cyclic order. Every pair of
internally disjoint endpoint chords that alternates in that order must cross.

The exact interval certificate exhausts all `6!` cyclic orders. For every order it finds
an alternating pair of internal trains whose projective direction gap is strictly less
than `pi/3` throughout the certified local angle box. Such a pair cannot cross in a
strict unit rhombus, by the audited train-crossing angle theorem. Therefore

\[
\boxed{
\text{the certified collapsed chamber has no common all-outer-host realization.}
}
\]

This is not an exclusion of the collapsed branch. It leaves open other points of the
two-parameter local family and all attachments to multiple activated hosts. It is a
global chamber reduction: any realization in this certified chamber must use at least
two distinct outside/activated host destinations.

## Exact finite argument

Use the twelve edge-angle intervals from the local contraction certificate. For a fixed
cyclic order of the six pentagon hosts, two internally disjoint trains with alternating
endpoints must cross as proper chords. The interval verifier checks every such pair.
For each of the 720 host orders, it records one alternating pair `(e,f)` and proves

\[
\operatorname{dist}_{\mathbb R/\pi\mathbb Z}(\theta_e,\theta_f)<\frac\pi3,
\]

using rational Taylor/Machin bounds for `pi` and rational interval arithmetic for the
angle box. The train-crossing theorem requires the opposite strict inequality
`dist > pi/3`, so the order is impossible. Since every host order is rejected, the
common-host subcase is excluded for the entire certified box.

The verifier does not assume that the local auxiliary genus-two side pairing is the
physical source. It uses only the common-host noncrossing consequence and the audited
proper-crossing angle threshold.

**Dependency chain:**

\[
\text{certified local chamber}
\to\text{parallel common-host trains are noncrossing}
\to\text{720 endpoint orders}
\to\text{angle-ineligible alternating pair in each order}
\to\text{common all-outer host excluded}.
\]

**Disposition:** exact chamber-local/global attachment exclusion; no theorem-state
change; positive-slack collapsed branch and C-II remain open.
